        "action": {
          "type": "get message",
          "content": "To solve the expression $((-7 + 9 \\times 9 \\times 6) - (-2 - -5 + 9 + -7))$, we will break it down into two main parts: the first term inside the outer parentheses and the second term inside the outer parentheses.\n\n**Step 1: Evaluate the first part $(-7 + 9 \\times 9 \\times 6)$**\nAccording to the order of operations (PEMDAS/BODMAS), multiplication comes before addition.\n1.  Calculate $9 \\times 9 = 81$.\n2.  Calculate $81 \\times 6 = 486$.\n3.  Now add $-7$: $-7 + 486 = 479$.\n\nSo, the first part equals **479**.\n\n**Step 2: Evaluate the second part $(-2 - -5 + 9 + -7)$**\nWe perform the operations from left to right. Note that subtracting a negative number is the same as adding a positive number ($ - -5 = +5 $).\n1.  Start with $-2 - -5$, which becomes $-2 + 5 = 3$.\n2.  Add 9: $3 + 9 = 12$.\n3.  Add $-7$: $12 + (-7) = 12 - 7 = 5$.\n\nSo, the second part equals **5**.\n\n**Step 3: Perform the final subtraction**\nNow substitute the values back into the original expression:\n$$ (479) - (5) $$\n$$ 479 - 5 = 474 $$\n\n**Answer:**\n474",
